@startuml
!include style/ail.puml
title Tutorial 4 · Callback timing: synthetic Watch registration and teardown
hide footbox
$caller(client, "Caller\nRegisters and destroys Watch")
$callee(api, "Label-card API\nRetains and invokes handler")
$callee(handler, "LabelChanged\nRuns one synchronous invocation")
note over client, api : watch_labels enters: borrow handler; retain for owner($result).
api -> client : watch_labels returns: give one owned Watch
note over api, handler
  Only now may invocations start: timing(after_return).
  count(0, many): zero delivery is permitted over the whole owner scope.
end note
api -[dotted]> handler : Start one permitted invocation with a scalar change number
activate handler
note over client, handler
  Caller enters destroy_watch outside this callback use.
  An invocation may still be in flight on another thread.
end note
client -> api : destroy_watch entry: consume Watch
note over handler : In-flight invocation completes during teardown.
deactivate handler
note over client, api : destroy_watch returns: no invocation running or able to start.
note over api, handler
  Quiescence is at destructor return, not entry.
  One permitted trace: no delivery or eventual-return guarantee.
  Watch retains no Card and extends no borrowed Label's validity.
end note
legend bottom
  Time runs downward; spacing is not duration. Native scheduling is omitted.
  Actor = caller; rectangles = callees (API becomes caller when invoking LabelChanged).
  Bar = active callback; dotted arrow = callback entry; solid arrow = ownership transfer.
  Notes mark other entries and completions; no arrow denotes an ordinary return.
  Non-normative: C3, C5, C6. scope(owner($result)); quiescence(@destroy_watch).
  requires(outside_callback(@watch_labels::handler)) excludes self-waiting teardown.
endlegend
@enduml
